**Diagnosis**
The issue is definitively a failure to release the lock in the exception handler of the cancel operation, not priority inversion. Priority inversion occurs when a high-priority thread is blocked by a lower-priority thread holding a lock, often exacerbated by a medium-priority thread preempting the low-priority one; this results in scheduling delays, not a permanent deadlock. In this scenario, both threads remain permanently blocked because the lock holder bypassed the manual unlock instruction when an exception was thrown during the cancellation event.

**Root Cause**
When the user cancels the 50GB update, the 'FileWriter' thread acquires the mutex but encounters a cancellation exception. The execution flow jumps to the catch block or returns early, skipping the manual `unlock()` call and leaving the mutex permanently locked. The 'NetworkManager' subsequently attempts to acquire the same mutex to clean up the connection and blocks forever, which in turn blocks the UI thread waiting for the NetworkManager to signal completion.

**Solution**
Enforce deterministic resource management using RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) patterns or a guaranteed `finally` block. This ensures the mutex is released automatically upon scope exit, regardless of exceptions or early returns.

**Pseudo-Code Fix**

```cpp
// Vulnerable Logic
void FileWriter::WriteChunk(Data* buf) {
    fileMutex.lock();
    try {
        if (cancelRequested) throw CancelException();
        WriteToFile(buf);
    } catch (...) {
        return; // Lock remains held, causing deadlock
    }
    fileMutex.unlock();
}

// Corrected Logic using RAII
void FileWriter::WriteChunk(Data* buf) {
    // Scoped lock automatically releases mutex via destructor
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(fileMutex); 

    try {
        if (cancelRequested) throw CancelException();
        WriteToFile(buf);
    } catch (...) {
        LogError("Write failed or cancelled");
        // Lock releases automatically here upon scope exit
        throw; 
    }
}

void NetworkManager::Cleanup() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(fileMutex);
    CloseConnection();
}
```

This approach guarantees the mutex is released deterministically upon cancellation, preventing the UI thread from hanging indefinitely.

