The model is fundamentally incompatible with Unity's Mecanim Humanoid rig without external structural modification. A single-bone spine cannot satisfy the inverse kinematics requirements for a standard three-bone chain (Hips, Spine, Chest). IK solvers rely on intermediate transforms to calculate bending angles and torso curvature; lacking these degrees of freedom causes procedural bending to fail. Furthermore, because the model fails Unity's Humanoid retargeting validation, the Mecanim system cannot properly map animation data to the skeleton. Programmatic retargeting cannot invent missing bone transforms at import time, meaning the asset must be rejected or re-rigged in a DCC tool.

To enforce this automatically during import, implement an `AssetPostprocessor` that inspects the imported hierarchy to ensure the spine chain meets the minimum depth requirement before allowing the asset into the project.

```csharp
using UnityEditor;
using UnityEngine;

public class RigValidationPostprocessor : AssetPostprocessor
{
    void OnPostprocessModel(GameObject g)
    {
        ModelImporter importer = assetImporter as ModelImporter;
        if (importer.animationType == ModelImporterAnimationType.Humanoid)
        {
            Transform hips = FindBone(g.transform, "Hips");
            if (hips == null || hips.childCount < 1)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"[Rig Audit] {assetPath} rejected: Missing required spine hierarchy for IK.");
                importer.animationType = ModelImporterAnimationType.None;
            }
        }
    }

    Transform FindBone(Transform root, string name)
    {
        foreach (Transform t in root.GetComponentsInChildren<Transform>())
            if (t.name.Contains(name)) return t;
        return null;
    }
}
```

This ensures only rigs with sufficient topology for IK calculations are accepted, preventing runtime failures.

